---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/CompressPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-pdf">
  <CompressPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to compress a PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above.</li>
        <li>
          Choose a mode. <strong>Quality presets</strong> render each page again as a JPEG at a set
          resolution, which gives the large savings. <strong>Lossless clean-up</strong> rebuilds the
          file structure and does not change the page content.
        </li>
        <li>Press <strong>Compress PDF</strong> and watch the per-page progress.</li>
        <li>Compare the before and after sizes, then download. If the file is not small enough, lower the DPI and run it again.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What each preset actually does</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[620px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Preset</th>
              <th scope="col">Resolution</th>
              <th scope="col">JPEG quality</th>
              <th scope="col">Best for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Strong</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">72 DPI</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">50</td>
              <td>Screen-only reading, email attachments with a hard size limit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Recommended</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">110 DPI</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">62</td>
              <td>The everyday choice: readable on any screen, still legible printed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Light</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">150 DPI</td>
              <td class="tabular-nums">80</td>
              <td>Documents that will be printed or archived</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Lossless clean-up</td>
              <td>unchanged</td>
              <td>n/a</td>
              <td>Text documents where search and copy must keep working</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        An A4 page at 110 DPI is about 900 × 1270 pixels, enough for body text to stay sharp on a laptop
        screen and on a phone. At 72 DPI it is 595 × 842 pixels, which looks fine at normal zoom but
        softens small print. Custom mode shows both settings, so you can aim at a specific size.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why some PDFs barely shrink</h2>
      <p>
        Images take up most of the size of almost all PDFs. A 40-page report exported from a word
        processor can be 300 KB in total, because text is stored as a few kilobytes of font references
        and drawing operators per page, and there is nothing left to remove. One scanned page from a
        phone camera can be 3 MB alone.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Scans and photos: expect 70-95% smaller with the Recommended preset.</li>
        <li>Slide decks with photos: usually 50-80% smaller.</li>
        <li>Text-only documents: a few percent at best, and rasterising them makes the file bigger. Use lossless clean-up.</li>
        <li>Already-compressed scans: if a scanner already saved JPEGs at 100 DPI, re-encoding gains little and costs quality.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Try greyscale on colour scans of black-and-white originals: removing the chroma channels usually
        saves another 20-30% with no change to readability.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The trade-off, stated plainly</h2>
      <p>
        Rasterising cannot be undone. After it, the page is a picture: you cannot select or search the
        text, links stop working, form fields are flattened, and screen readers cannot read the content.
        All "extreme compression" options work this way. If any of this matters, keep the original, and
        use the lossless mode when the document must stay accessible.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
